# *.sh must stay LF no matter what checks it out or on what platform — a CRLF shebang
# line breaks with "bad interpreter" on Linux, and these specifically exist to run on
# the Pi 5 hub. core.autocrlf=true on a Windows dev machine would otherwise convert them
# to CRLF on checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning as the .sh rule above, and just as load-bearing: a systemd unit checked
# out with CRLF puts a stray  on the end of the last argument of every directive, so
# ExecStart's final flag silently gains a carriage return and the service fails in a way
# that reads as a bad flag rather than a line-ending problem. These units are written on
# Windows and run only on the Pi, so they cross the boundary every time.
*.service text eol=lf

# Cosmetic symmetry, not load-bearing the way the .sh rule is: PowerShell tolerates LF
# fine, but keep .ps1 native-Windows CRLF since that's the platform they're for.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
